WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.540 --> 00:00:07.980
We continue to add features to NCBI's genome
browser, the Genome Data Viewer.

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:13.840
A recent addition is the BLAST widget, which
we'll explore in the next two minutes.

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:17.390
You may be surprised to learn that a good
place to start your BLAST search is right

00:00:17.390 --> 00:00:19.990
on the Genome Date Viewer home page.

00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:24.300
Here you can choose the exact assembly version
that you want to search.

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:29.270
Keep in mind that this BLAST interface searches
the top-level sequences that are part of RefSeq

00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:39.020
genome assemblies, which number well over
500 eukaryotic genomes.

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:43.090
Once you have your BLAST results, you will
see a link to Genome Data Viewer on the right

00:00:43.090 --> 00:00:44.810
side of the Alignments section.

00:00:44.810 --> 00:00:50.100
You may also see a link to MapViewer, but
we will eventually remove that.

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:55.780
The GDV Ideogram shows you the distribution
of BLAST hits across the genome; notice the

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:56.940
arrowheads.

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:02.690
The best alignment is represented by the darkest
arrowhead in this case on chromosome 15, which

00:01:02.690 --> 00:01:07.360
is the alignment that I selected in the BLAST
results, and that sequence is presented in

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:10.810
the sequence viewer portion of the GDV.

00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:14.850
This is where your BLAST alignments show up
in BLAST tracks.

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:20.170
The best part is, you now have the full functionality
of the Genome Data Viewer, including the ability

00:01:20.170 --> 00:01:26.140
to add your own track data, and a new feature,
the BLAST Alignment Inspector, which can be

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:28.640
launched from within the BLAST widget.

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:37.430
The Inspector allows you to easily examine
BLAST alignments in a table view, or graphically,

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:43.100
all the way down to the sequence level.

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:49.310
My query in this case was a mouse cDNA, so
we see many mismatches in red.

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:55.229
The BLAST widget offers another way to load
BLAST results, by entering a BLAST RID number.

00:01:55.229 --> 00:01:59.880
In the Alignment section of the widget you
can sort the alignments, and zoom to one or

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:12.270
more selected alignments, add a marker, and
filter by percent identity.

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:16.569
You can also initiate a new BLAST search from
here.

00:02:16.569 --> 00:02:21.659
Give the Genome Data Viewer a try the next
time you run BLAST against assembled genomes.

